# Wintermute fleet agent environment, read by systemd as an EnvironmentFile.
#
# Not a shell script: systemd takes each line literally, so do not quote values
# unless you want the quotes, and do not use $VAR expansion.
#
# Every variable the agent reads is listed here, set to its default. An empty
# value is the same as not setting it at all — the agent trims and falls back —
# so this file as it stands is the stock configuration, and nothing here needs
# commenting out to get the default behaviour. Only WINTERMUTE_SERVER and
# WINTERMUTE_TOKEN must be filled in.
#
# Each setting comes first and the paragraph explaining it sits underneath: the
# name is what you are scanning for, the prose is what you read once you have
# found it.
#
# The installer writes /etc/wintermute/node.env for you, with the server, the
# token and anything passed as --store or --runtime, and leaves that file alone
# on every later update. This copy sits beside it as the reference for the rest:
# edit the real one to change what the agent does, then restart the service.
#
# Filling one in by hand is still fine — mode 600, owned by the service user,
# since it contains a token.

# ---- required --------------------------------------------------------------

WINTERMUTE_SERVER=

# The server this host reports to. Include the scheme and the port.
# No default; the agent refuses to start without it.


WINTERMUTE_TOKEN=

# This node's client token, issued on the server with:
#
#   wintermuted -add-client rig-01 -kind node
#
# The name given there is how this machine is identified everywhere — in the
# fleet view, and in the model assignments. It is never taken from the hostname
# the agent reports, because a node that could name itself could write telemetry
# attributed to another one.
#
# Shown once and stored only as a hash. Issue a new one if it is lost.
# No default; the agent refuses to start without it.

# ---- reporting -------------------------------------------------------------

WINTERMUTE_NODE_INTERVAL=15s
WINTERMUTE_NODE_PUSH=1m

# How often to take a reading, and how often to send what has been collected.
# Collecting faster than sending is the normal arrangement; a push shorter than
# the interval is raised to the interval rather than sending a reading twice.
# Go duration strings — 15s, 1m, 2h30m. An unparseable value falls back to the
# default rather than failing to start.


WINTERMUTE_NODE_SPOOL=

# Where readings that could not be delivered are held across a restart, so an
# outage is not a gap in the series. Written only when a send fails: a healthy
# agent never touches it.
#
# Empty means the state directory systemd hands the unit — with StateDirectory=
# as shipped, /var/lib/wintermute-node/spool.json, created and owned by the
# service user before the agent starts. Run outside systemd, with no
# STATE_DIRECTORY set, it falls back to $HOME/.cache/wintermute-node/spool.json.
#
# Set it only to put the spool somewhere else; anywhere outside StateDirectory
# needs a matching ReadWritePaths= in the unit.

# ---- the model store -------------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything below is optional. With all of it empty this agent reports host
# metrics and nothing else, which is a complete configuration.

WINTERMUTE_NODE_STORE=

# Where this host keeps its own copy of the weights it has been assigned, so
# switching a model is a local file read rather than a download. Empty means
# this node keeps no store and only reports.
#
# Created if missing. Needs room for whole models — tens of gigabytes — so it is
# usually not on the root filesystem. A path outside StateDirectory needs a
# matching ReadWritePaths= in the unit.
#
# The agent needs no share, NFS or Samba: left to itself it fetches over HTTP
# from the server, resumably, authenticating with the token above. When a share
# is available, WINTERMUTE_NODE_REPO_MOUNT below is the way to use it.
#
# Pointing this straight at a read-only mount of the server's repository also
# works, and is a different trade: nothing is copied to this host at all, so a
# node holds no weights of its own and every load reads over the network. With
# llamacpp that is what gets served; ollama still imports into its blob store,
# which is local. Add --no-mmap to WINTERMUTE_NODE_LLAMA_SERVER_ARGS if you do
# it — llama.cpp's mmap is pathological over a network filesystem — and note
# that a read-only store needs no ReadWritePaths in the unit, since nothing
# writes to it.


WINTERMUTE_NODE_REPO_MOUNT=

# A local mount of the server's model repository — the same directory the
# server serves over HTTP, exported over Samba or NFS and mounted here. When an
# assignment is found there it is copied instead of downloaded, which on a LAN
# is several times faster and keeps the transfer off the server's own HTTP path.
#
# It is an optimisation and never a requirement. An empty value, a share that is
# not mounted, one that does not hold this particular model, and one whose copy
# fails the digest the server published all fall back to downloading — so a new
# node works before anyone has set up a share, and an existing node carries on
# when the share is down. A copy that could not be used is said once on stderr
# rather than passed over silently, because the symptom otherwise is only that
# transfers got slower.
#
# The copy is verified against the assignment exactly as a download is. A share
# is a directory some other process writes, so what is on it may be half-written
# or may be a different model under the same name; local is not trusted.
#
# Mount it read-only — the agent only ever reads here — and, since this is
# usually a network mount that appears late, name it in the unit as well:
#
#   //wintermute/models  /mnt/wintermute-repo  cifs  credentials=…,ro,_netdev  0 0
#   RequiresMountsFor=/mnt/wintermute-repo
#
# ProtectSystem=strict does not block reads, so no ReadWritePaths is needed.


WINTERMUTE_NODE_RUNTIME=

# What actually serves models on this host: ollama, llamacpp, or empty to keep
# the files and wire them up by hand. Anything else is rejected at startup.
#
# It decides what happens after a file arrives. llama.cpp takes the path, so
# nothing is copied. Ollama imports into its own content-addressed blob store,
# so every model occupies disk twice on those hosts — once in the store above
# and once inside Ollama. That is a property of Ollama, not a defect here.


WINTERMUTE_NODE_OLLAMA_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434

# With ollama: the local Ollama to import into. The agent talks to its HTTP API
# and never runs the ollama binary.


WINTERMUTE_NODE_RUNTIME_URL=

# Where this host's runtime serves, as something other than this host would
# reach it. Reported to the server so the Models screen can offer to declare a
# backend on this node instead of making you type the address.
#
# It is only ever a suggestion: the server rewrites a loopback address to this
# host's reported hostname, shows it, and probes it before a backend is stored.
# So the ordinary Ollama case needs nothing here — empty falls through to the
# URL above, and the server turns it into http://<this host>:11434. Set it when
# the port differs, when llama-swap is what serves (the agent writes its config
# and never speaks to it, so it cannot know), or when this machine is reached by
# a name other than the one it reports.


WINTERMUTE_NODE_LLAMA_SWAP_CONFIG=

# With llamacpp: the llama-swap config this agent owns and rewrites in full
# whenever the store changes.
#
# Do not hand-edit it and do not point this at a config you maintain yourself —
# it is regenerated, not merged. Keep your own models in your own config and
# give the fleet-managed set this file. Run llama-swap with --watch-config so it
# reloads when the agent writes.


WINTERMUTE_NODE_LLAMA_SERVER=llama-server
WINTERMUTE_NODE_LLAMA_SERVER_ARGS=

# The binary named in that generated config, and the flags appended to every
# generated command — the ones that are a property of this host rather than of
# any model, e.g. --n-gpu-layers 99.
